  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00037` — `oversized_raster`, `src/assets/hero-banner.png`, severity **low**, confidence **0.95**, effort **quick**.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)`\n> - `severity ladder: low ≥ 200 KB · medium ≥ 500 KB · high ≥ 1000 KB`\n> - `format: png`\n> - `consider resizing to the rendered dimensions and re-encoding to webp or avif`\n\nTwo things the evidence does **not** give you: pixel dimensions, and where the asset renders. I read the first off the file directly — `1000 x 1000, 8-bit/color RGB, non-interlaced`. The second isn't knowable from this repo (nothing in the fixture references `hero-banner`), so I'm taking the PR description's word that it's the landing hero.\n\n## Block or request changes\n\n**Request changes. Don't block.**\n\n253.9 KB is 27% over the *lowest* rung of a three-rung ladder — it's nowhere near `medium` (500 KB), let alone `high` (1000 KB). `agent_risk` is 0.05 and `effort` is `quick`. Holding a branch over a low-severity finding with a quick fix is disproportionate, and the detector itself is only claiming \"this is heavier than a budget,\" not \"this is broken.\"\n\nTwo caveats that push it up from \"nit\" to \"fix before merge\":\n\n1. **The ladder is render-blind.** It scores bytes, not position in the critical path. A 254 KB hero is materially worse than a 254 KB below-the-fold illustration, because the hero is almost certainly the LCP element. The finding under-states this specific case, and that's a limit of the detector, not a reason to discount it.\n2. **This is a new asset.** Cheapest possible moment to fix it is before it lands and starts getting referenced.\n\nSeparately: if this PR touches `src/assets/` generally, `crime_00035` (`svg_with_embedded_raster` on `partner-logo.svg`, severity **high**, confidence 0.95) is the finding in that directory I'd actually escalate. The hero is the loud one; that one's the real defect.\n\n## What the byte count tells you about the content\n\n260,000 bytes ÷ 1,000,000 pixels = **0.26 bytes/pixel, lossless**. A photograph at 1000×1000 in PNG would be 1–2 MB. At 0.26 B/px this is flat colour regions, gradients, or limited palette — synthetic/illustrative artwork, not a photo. That drives both recommendations below.\n\n## Target dimensions\n\nThe source is **square (1000×1000)**, which is unusual for something called a banner. Resolve this first, because it changes the answer:\n\n- **If it renders full-bleed as a wide banner**, the CSS is cropping a square source, and you are shipping pixels that are decoded and then thrown away. Re-export at the actual aspect ratio — that alone is the single biggest win, before any format change.\n- **If it renders as a contained square/portrait panel**, 1000×1000 is only ~1.67× a 600 CSS px box — already short of a clean 2× for retina, so don't downscale the source further than you need to.\n\nDon't ship one fixed size either way. Emit a `srcset` ladder and let the browser pick:\n\n```html\n<img\n  srcset=\"hero-banner-480.avif 480w,\n          hero-banner-768.avif 768w,\n          hero-banner-1000.avif 1000w\"\n  sizes=\"(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 600px\"\n  ...\n>\n```\n\nCap at 1000w — that's the source, and upscaling buys nothing. Set the `sizes` value from the measured rendered box, not from a guess.\n\n## WebP vs AVIF\n\n**AVIF, with a WebP fallback in `<picture>`.** If your build pipeline can't emit two formats, ship WebP alone and move on — the resize is doing most of the work regardless.\n\nWhy AVIF for *this* image specifically:\n\n- At equal perceptual quality AVIF typically runs 20–35% smaller than WebP on photographic content, and the gap **widens** on synthetic/flat content — which the 0.26 B/px figure says this is.\n- WebP's VP8 8×8 block structure bands visibly on large smooth gradients. AVIF doesn't. A gradient-heavy brand hero at hero scale is exactly where that artifact is most noticeable.\n- Browser support is no longer the tiebreaker it was in 2022; Safari, Chrome, Firefox and Edge all decode AVIF. The `<picture>` fallback covers the remainder at zero cost.\n\nOne caveat: **if the hero contains rendered text or a sharp-edged logo**, lossy encoding at q≈60–75 will ring on those edges. In that case use AVIF *lossless*, or keep PNG and run it through `oxipng`/`pngquant` — a lossless PNG this compressible often gives up another 30–50% to a palette quantizer with no visible change.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nResize to 768×768 (59% of the pixels) plus AVIF at q≈60–65 should land somewhere in the **20–45 KB** range — roughly an 85% cut, and comfortably under the 200 KB threshold so the finding clears.\n\nTreat that range as a prediction, not a result. Encode it and check the actual bytes before you close the review comment.",
